Q: codeigniter Active Record : Fields lost when performing a JOIN A: <p>I have same your error. And I find the solution for this issue: </p>

<pre><code>        === the query ===
    $this-&gt;db-&gt;select('messages.*');
    $this-&gt;db-&gt;select('exp.id as exp_id, exp.firstname as exp_firstname, exp.lastname as exp_lastname');
    $this-&gt;db-&gt;select('dest.id as dest_id, dest.firstname as dest_firstname, dest.lastname as dest_lastname');
    $this-&gt;db-&gt;where('messages.id', 999);
    $this-&gt;db-&gt;join('users as exp', 'users.id=messages.exp_id', 'INNER');
    $this-&gt;db-&gt;join('users as dest', 'users.id=messages.dest_id', 'INNER');
    $res = $this-&gt;db-&gt;get('messages');
</code></pre>

<p>So the result could look like this:</p>

<pre><code>id    exp_id  dest_id    content      exp_id  exp_firstname exp_lastname   dest_id    dest_firstname     dest_lastname
999     1       2       Hello Graig!    1       Bob             Smith         2          Graig              Anderson
</code></pre>

Q: How to configure WolverineFx in a background worker project A: <p>Fix startup order: Register Wolverine before your HostedService</p>
<p>You are hitting a race condition because <code>IHostedService</code> implementations start in the order they are added to the DI container.</p>
<p>In your code, you registered <code>AddHostedService&lt;Worker&gt;()</code> <em>before</em> calling <code>UseWolverine()</code>. This means your Worker attempts to start and use the <code>IMessageBus</code> before Wolverine's own runtime has finished initializing.</p>
<p>Swap the order so Wolverine initializes first:</p>
<pre><code>var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// 1. Configure Wolverine first so its services are ready
builder.UseWolverine(opts =&gt; {
    opts.CodeGeneration.TypeLoadMode = TypeLoadMode.Dynamic;
});

// 2. Then register your worker
// This ensures Wolverine is fully started before your Worker.ExecuteAsync runs
builder.Services.AddHostedService&lt;Worker&gt;(); 

var host = builder.Build();
host.Run();
</code></pre>
<p>Also, ensure you are using the <code>IMessageBus</code> inside <code>ExecuteAsync</code> (or <code>StartAsync</code>), <strong>not</strong> in the constructor. The bus isn't ready until the host actually runs.</p>
